.TH SLS 1 "October 2026" "sls 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sls \- remote directory listing via SSH (scp-style)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sls
[\fIssh-options\fR] [\fIls-options\fR] [\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR:\fIpath\fR
.br
.B sls
[\fIssh-options\fR] [\fIls-options\fR] [\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sls
lists the contents of a remote directory over SSH using the same
.IR user@host:path
syntax as
.BR scp (1).
.PP
SSH connection options are recognized and passed to
.BR ssh (1).
All remaining options are passed through to the remote
.BR ls (1).
.PP
Authentication and connection details are handled entirely by the
system
.BR ssh (1)
client, which automatically reads
.BR ~/.ssh/config
(including
.BR Port ,
.BR User ,
.BR IdentityFile ,
host aliases,
.BR ProxyJump ,
etc.).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS SSH options
.TP
.BR -P \fIport\fR
Connect to the remote host on the specified port
(overrides any Port in the config).
.TP
.BR -i \fIidentity_file\fR
Identity (private key) file.
.TP
.BR -o \fIssh_option\fR
Pass an extra option to ssh in the format used by
.BR ssh_config (5).
May be repeated.
.TP
.BR -F \fIssh_config\fR
Use an alternate per-user SSH configuration file.
.TP
.BR -J \fIdestination\fR
Connect via the given ProxyJump host.
.TP
.BR -4
Force IPv4.
.TP
.BR -6
Force IPv6.
.TP
.BR -C
Enable compression.
.TP
.BR -v
Verbose mode (debug output from ssh).
.TP
.BR -q
Quiet mode.
.SS ls options
.TP
.I ls-options
Any options accepted by the remote
.BR ls (1)
(for example
.BR -l ,
.BR -a ,
.BR -h ,
.BR -R ,
.BR --color=auto ).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
List a remote directory in long format:
.B sls -l user@example.com:/var/log
.TP
Non-standard port and specific key:
.B sls -P 2222 -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 -la host:.
.TP
Disable host key checking for this call:
.B sls -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no -h user@host
.TP
Via a jump host:
.B sls -J bastion.example.com -la internal:/var
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status is that of the remote
.BR ls (1)
(or of
.BR ssh (1)
on connection failure).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR scp (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5)
